    {
      "@id": "concept:human-rights-legislation-amendment-act-2017-sch-2-sec-46pk",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "46PK Proceedings at conferences",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/human-rights-legislation-amendment-act-2017#sch-2-sec-46pk"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "human-rights-legislation-amendment-act-2017.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "human-rights-legislation-amendment-act-2017.pdf, p.18",
      "values:originalText": "(1) Subject to this section, a conference mentioned in\nsubsection 46PJ(1) is to be conducted in such manner as the person\npresiding at the conference considers appropriate.\n\n(2) The conference is to be conducted in private.\n\n(3) The person presiding at the conference must take all reasonable\nsteps to ensure that the conduct of the conference does not\ndisadvantage any complainant or respondent.\n\n(4) Unless the person presiding at the conference consents:\n\n(a) an individual is not entitled to be represented at the\nconference by another person; and\n\n(b) a body (whether or not incorporated) is not entitled to be\nrepresented at the conference otherwise than by a person who\nis an officer or employee of the body.\n\nAmendment of the Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986  Schedule 2\n\nHuman Rights Legislation Amendment Act 2017\nCompilation date: 01/09/2021\nRegistered: 03/11/2021\n\n(5) Despite paragraph (4)(a), an individual who is unable to attend the\nconference because the individual has a disability is entitled to\nnominate another person to attend instead on his or her behalf.\n\n(6) If the person presiding at the conference considers that an\nindividual is unable to participate fully in the conference because\nthe individual has a disability, the individual is entitled to nominate\nanother person to assist him or her at the conference.\n\n(7) For the purposes of this section, disability has the same meaning as\nin the Disability Discrimination Act 1992.\n46PKA  Things said in conciliation are not admissible in evidence in\ncertain proceedings\n\n(1) Evidence of anything said or done by a person in the course of the\nconciliation of a complaint in accordance with section 46PF is not\nadmissible in any proceedings relating to the alleged acts,\nomissions or practices.\n\n(2) Subsection (1) does not apply for the purposes of the application of\nsection 46PSA.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) Subject to this section, a conference mentioned in\nsubsection 46PJ(1) is to be conducted in such manner as the person\npresiding at the conference considers appropriate.\n\n(2) The conference is to be conducted in private.\n\n(3) The person presiding at the conference must take all reasonable\nsteps to ensure that the conduct of the conference does not\ndisadvantage any complainant or respondent.\n\n(4) Unless the person presiding at the conference consents:\n\n(a) an individual is not entitled to be represented at the\nconference by another person; and\n\n(b) a body (whether or not incorporated) is not entitled to be\nrepresented at the conference otherwise than by a person who\nis an officer or employee of the body.\n\nAmendment of the Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986  Schedule 2\n\nHuman Rights Legislation Amendment Act 2017\nCompilation date: 01/09/2021\nRegistered: 03/11/2021\n\n(5) Despite paragraph (4)(a), an individual who is unable to attend the\nconference because the individual has a disability is entitled to\nnominate another person to attend instead on his or her behalf.\n\n(6) If the person presiding at the conference considers that an\nindividual is unable to participate fully in the conference because\nthe individual has a disability, the individual is entitled to nominate\nanother person to assist him or her at the conference.\n\n(7) For the purposes of this section, disability has the same meaning as\nin the Disability Discrimination Act 1992.\n46PKA  Things said in conciliation are not admissible in evidence in\ncertain proceedings\n\n(1) Evidence of anything said or done by a person in the course of the\nconciliation of a complaint in accordance with section 46PF is not\nadmissible in any proceedings relating to the alleged acts,\nomissions or practices.\n\n(2) Subsection (1) does not apply for the purposes of the application of\nsection 46PSA.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:human-rights-legislation-amendment-act-2017-sch-2-sec-46pk-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:human-rights-legislation-amendment-act-2017-sch-2-sec-46pk-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:human-rights-legislation-amendment-act-2017-sch-2-sec-46pk-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:human-rights-legislation-amendment-act-2017-sch-2-sec-46pk-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:human-rights-legislation-amendment-act-2017-sch-2-sec-46pk-ss-5"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:human-rights-legislation-amendment-act-2017-sch-2-sec-46pk-ss-6"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:human-rights-legislation-amendment-act-2017-sch-2-sec-46pk-ss-7"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:human-rights-legislation-amendment-act-2017-sch-2-sec-46pk-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:human-rights-legislation-amendment-act-2017-sch-2-sec-46pk-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "822a1b1c57011deac912747dd6c876cbb1c8f5b5c7f3726a09e59f9bcb5b3944"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/human-rights-legislation-amendment-act-2017#sch-2-sec-46pk",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "46PK Proceedings at conferences",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 18,
      "values:originalText": "(1) Subject to this section, a conference mentioned in\nsubsection 46PJ(1) is to be conducted in such manner as the person\npresiding at the conference considers appropriate.\n\n(2) The conference is to be conducted in private.\n\n(3) The person presiding at the conference must take all reasonable\nsteps to ensure that the conduct of the conference does not\ndisadvantage any complainant or respondent.\n\n(4) Unless the person presiding at the conference consents:\n\n(a) an individual is not entitled to be represented at the\nconference by another person; and\n\n(b) a body (whether or not incorporated) is not entitled to be\nrepresented at the conference otherwise than by a person who\nis an officer or employee of the body.\n\nAmendment of the Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986  Schedule 2\n\nHuman Rights Legislation Amendment Act 2017\nCompilation date: 01/09/2021\nRegistered: 03/11/2021\n\n(5) Despite paragraph (4)(a), an individual who is unable to attend the\nconference because the individual has a disability is entitled to\nnominate another person to attend instead on his or her behalf.\n\n(6) If the person presiding at the conference considers that an\nindividual is unable to participate fully in the conference because\nthe individual has a disability, the individual is entitled to nominate\nanother person to assist him or her at the conference.\n\n(7) For the purposes of this section, disability has the same meaning as\nin the Disability Discrimination Act 1992.\n46PKA  Things said in conciliation are not admissible in evidence in\ncertain proceedings\n\n(1) Evidence of anything said or done by a person in the course of the\nconciliation of a complaint in accordance with section 46PF is not\nadmissible in any proceedings relating to the alleged acts,\nomissions or practices.\n\n(2) Subsection (1) does not apply for the purposes of the application of\nsection 46PSA."
    },
    {
